Q: Asynchronous request and insertion of received nodes A: <p>It seems to me that you want a function to create an HTML element with some synchronously determined attributes (like the <code>className</code> or <code>textContent</code>) and some asynchronously determined children (for example, the result of HTTP requests).</p>
<p>Here is a simplified working example that illustrates what I mean. It creates a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element with synchronously determined <code>text</code> &quot;Your IP address&quot; and adds one piece of <code>data</code>, which is another such <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> element (obtained by recursive invocation of the function) whose <code>text</code> is your IP address as determined by an HTTP request.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>function divWithContent(text, ...data) {
  const div = document.createElement("div");
  div.textContent = text;
  for (const d of data)
    d.then(r =&gt; div.appendChild(r));
  return div;
}
document.body.appendChild(divWithContent(
  "Your IP address",
  fetch("https://httpbin.org/ip")
  .then(
    r =&gt; r.json()
  ).then(
    j =&gt; divWithContent(j.origin)
  )
));</code></pre>
